Q: Passing the size of a VLA as a pointer reference A: <p>In this function declaration</p>
<pre><code>void foo(largeStructType* largeStructPtr, int arr[largeStructPtr-&gt;size])
</code></pre>
<p>the declaration of the second parameter indeed declares a variable length array because 1) the array declaration is not incomplete (for a parameter of an incomplete  variable length array in a function prototype there is used the symbol <strong>'*'</strong>) and 2) has a non-constant expression.</p>
<p>It is unimportant that the compiler will adjust the parameter to a pointer to the array element type. Nevertheless the declaration itself declares a variable length array.</p>
<p>An implementation may conditionally support variable length arrays.</p>
<p>Here is a demonstration program.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;


void display( size_t, int [*] );
void display( size_t n, int a[n] )
{
    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%d &quot;, a[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );
}

int main(void) 
{
    int a[] = { 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
    display( sizeof( a ) / sizeof( *a ), a );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Its output is</p>
<pre><code>0 1 2 3 4 5 
</code></pre>
<p>As you can see the function parameter in the both function declarations (that declare the same one function)  is declared as a variable length array. Even if the first function declaration will be removed nevertheless in the second function declaration the second function parameter still declares a variable length array.</p>

Q: Comparing two byte arrays in .NET A: <p>Options, I use extensions..  Very fast, but have never used it against a 3GB byte array, as an example.</p>
<p>EDIT: This works for all .NET versions like netstandard2.0 when .SequenceEquals and  ReadOnlySpan&lt;byte&gt; are not available.  Thanks <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/4489/hafthor" title="17,049 reputation">Hafthor</a></p>
<pre><code>internal static class MemoryExtensions
{
    /// &lt;summary&gt;
    /// Validates current byte array against another and returns if the bytes are identical in each or not.
    /// &lt;code&gt;
    /// var byteArray1 = new byte[2] {71, 97}
    /// var byteArray2 = new byte[2] {71, 97}
    /// if (byteArray1.EqualTo(byteArray2)) 
    ///     Console.WriteLine(&quot;Identical byte array&quot;);
    /// &lt;/code&gt;
    /// &lt;/summary&gt;
    /// &lt;param name=&quot;byteArr2&quot;&gt;Second byte array to compare with&lt;/param&gt;
    /// &lt;returns&gt;true: each byte in the array is identical.  false: the two arrays are not identical.&lt;/returns&gt;
    public static bool EqualTo(this byte[] @this, byte[] byteArr2) =&gt; @this.Where((x, i) =&gt; i &lt; byteArr2.Length &amp;&amp; x != byteArr2[i]).Count().Equals(0);
}
</code></pre>
